# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings, decided once instead of per machine.
#
# Without this file, git on Windows checks files out as CRLF while the content
# in history is LF. Every tool that reads a file then sees a different byte
# sequence depending on who cloned it -- which breaks byte-level checks (the
# upstream project has an ASCII-only rule, and this repo keeps it) and produces
# diffs that show every line changed for no reason.
#
# `text=auto` lets git normalise on commit and check out per platform;
# the explicit `eol=lf` entries pin the files where a stray CR would be a bug
# rather than a preference.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto

*.rs    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf

# Scripts too, and for a sharper reason than tidiness. A PowerShell script with
# CRLF works; the same script after a clone on a machine that normalises the other
# way does not, and the failure is a parse error on a line that looks correct.
# Adding an extension to this list is also the moment to check whether the file
# it covers is ASCII, which rule 7 requires and nothing else enforces for a shell
# script.
*.ps1   text eol=lf
*.cmd   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# Workflow files, for the same reason as the scripts: the thing that reads them is not on this
# machine. GitHub parses these bytes; a CR that a clone introduced would be a character in a
# `run:` body, and a shell does not forgive those. They are the one kind of file here that is
# executed somewhere nobody can look at it.
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Binary files: never touch them, never diff them, never try to merge them.
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.zip   binary
*.png   binary
